<DocLayout title="A real update, end to end" eyebrow="Reproducible walkthrough" description="Run the durable local Service, acknowledge a real deletion and insertion, query committed versions, and reopen the store." next={{label:"Production contracts", href:"/production/"}}>
  <p>No browser simulation. This trace is produced by the installed Python package and verified by the site’s example checks. The graph is an eight-vertex degree-four ring; it is not a throughput benchmark.</p>
  <div class="trace"><Replay /></div>
  <h2>Run the same path</h2>
  <p>Install from source with a C++17 compiler on a supported local POSIX environment. The temporary directory below deliberately isolates this disposable example; use a persistent private directory in your application.</p>
  <pre data-verify="python" data-copy={example}><code>{example}</code></pre>
  <h2>What is actually checked?</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Read vertex zero’s current partner, delete that matched edge, and wait for durable acknowledgment. The version advances once and the matching audit passes.</li>
    <li>Reinsert the edge. The version advances again; the matching audit passes, without assuming which valid maximal matching is selected.</li>
    <li>Close and reopen the SQLite store. All eight versioned partner results and every possible edge match the committed pre-close snapshot. Retrying request two returns its original outcome without another mutation; changing its payload rejects.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>SQLite commit precedes successful receipt completion. A receipt timeout does not cancel accepted work. Multi-query snapshots and arbitrary network storage are not implied by this example.</p>
</DocLayout>
